print("Hello Worlds")

doc:
    We should think about the variable's mutability and their conceptual notation.
    In onyx, the variable mutation system is based on the theory of linear types.

n: int = 7

print("There are {n} days in a week") (< The concept of string formatting will be explained later >)

x: int = ~n (< n transfers its value to x and becomes unavailable >)

(< x is never used: warning >)

(< --- >)

(< Sequences and iteration (Preview) >)

doc:
    A for loop is an iterator construct. It obtains one value at a time from
    its source and executes its body once for each obtained value.

    seq[T] is lazy: it computes its next value only when a for loop requests
    it. A seq has no indexing ([]), .length, or .shape; those belong to arr.

    A for loop consumes a seq. Once a seq has been traversed, it is no longer
    available. A for loop only reads an arr, so the arr remains available after
    the traversal.

numbers: arr[int] <3> = [1, 2, 3]

for number in numbers:
    print(number)

print(numbers) (< Valid: a for loop reads an arr without consuming it >)

(< bseq, seq construction, token, and the exact syntax of for sources are
   intentionally left open until the sequence contract is fully documented. >)
